 - **[Tailwind CSS](https://tailwindcss.com/)**: For styling the application.
 - **[Astro DB](https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/database/)**: Powers the comments feature, using a libSQL-compatible database.
+
+## WebMCP
+
+In a browser with WebMCP enabled, the site registers these client-side tools:
+
+- `search_articles({ query })`: case-insensitive title and summary search, with article URLs.
+- `get_reading_list({})`: saved articles for the current browser session.
+- `update_reading_list({ slug, action })`: explicitly `add` or `remove` an article. Both actions are idempotent.
+- `open_search({})` and `open_reading_list({})`: open a panel and wait until its form is available.
+- `read_current_article({})`: title, summary, visible body text and URL; available only on article pages.
+
+Opening the panels exposes the declarative `search_articles_form` and
+`manage_reading_list_form` tools. Their named fields define the parameters;
+submission uses the same logic as ordinary UI interactions and returns results
+through the agent's submit event. Closing a panel removes its form tool.
+Imperative tools follow the header island's mount/unmount lifecycle, including
+Astro client navigation. Reading-list updates synchronize mounted bookmark controls.
+
+WebMCP is experimental and requires a supporting browser configuration. The
+JavaScript integration detects the browser API; ordinary search and bookmarking
+also work without it. No MCP server or polyfill is required. See Chrome's
+[imperative API](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/ai/webmcp/imperative-api) and
+[declarative API](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/ai/webmcp/declarative-api) documentation.
